The Macedonians held a referendum on changing the country’s name.
The Macedonian people held a referendum on Sunday to change the country's name to "Republic of North Macedonia" to resolve a decade-old controversy with Greece, which had blocked its candidacy for membership in the European Union and NATO.
Greece, which has a state called Macedonia, voted to join NATO and the EU, saying that the people in the northern part of the country were advocating for its territory.
The two-pronged government signed an agreement in June based on a newly proposed name, but nationalist opponents argue that the change would destroy the ethnic identity of Macedonia’s main Slavic population.
President Georgi Ivanov did not vote in a referendum, and a boycott campaign casts doubt on whether voter turnout would meet at least 50 per per cent when the referendum takes effect.
Read questions about the referendum: “Are you in favor of accepting an agreement with Greece to become a member of NATO and the EU?”
Proponents of renaming, including Prime Minister Zoran Zayev, argue that it is worthwhile for Macedonia, one of the countries that have emerged since the fall of Yugoslavia, joining institutions such as the EU and NATO.
"I came here for the young people of Macedonia today to vote for the future of the country, so they will be able to live freely under the umbrella of the European Union, because it means a safer life for all of us," Skopje's Olivera Georgijevska, 79-year-old from Skopje in Skopje.
It is not legally binding, but he said that a sufficient number of people in parliament will comply with the results of the vote for a decision.
A name change would require a two-thirds majority in parliament.
