Vladimir Kavardarkov, a 54-year-old in front of a parliament in Skopje drew a chair in front of a tent set up by people preparing a small stage and boycotting the referendum.
Kavadarkov said, "We are in favour of NATO and the EU, but not through the service door, but not through a service door," Kavadarkov said.
“We’re a poor country, but it’s dignified.
“If they don’t accept us as Macedonia, we can rely on other countries such as China and Russia to become part of European-Asian integration.”
Zayev said NATO membership would provide the necessary investment for Macedonia, where the unemployment rate exceeds twenty per cent.
"More than eighty per cent of the population supports the EU and Nato, so I believe the majority will support it," Zayev said after the vote.
He said the "Jesus" result would be "a proof of our future."
According to a public opinion released last Monday according to a Macedonian policy research institute, 30 to 43 per cent of voters said they would take part in a referendum - below the voter turnout that is needed.
In a separate public opinion held by Thelma television in Macedonia, 57 per cent of respondents said they planned to go to the polls on Sunday.
Seventy per per cent of them said they would vote for consent.
Fifty per cent and one vote are needed for the referendum to be successful.
