John-Edward: The essential language skills of a global citizen
Scotland's private schools maintain an academically outstanding track record, and this is another set of excellent exam results that are reinforced only by individual and collective success in the achievements of the sport, the arts, music and other communities.
These schools, represented by the Scottish Council of Independent Schools (SCIS) with more than 30,000 pupils across Scotland, are committed to providing the highest level of service to their students and parents.
Private schools aim to equip their students with higher education, the careers of their choice, as well as living in society as global citizens.
In this educational sector, which can design and implement custom-made school curricula, we can see that the modern language continues to be the school's favorite subject.
Nelson Mandela said, "If you speak in a language you can understand, it's going into a person's head."
If you speak in that person's native language, it enters the person's heart."
This reminds us that we should not rely solely on English when building relationships and trust with people in other countries.
This year’s most recent exam results show that the language is at the top of the league table, with the highest pass rate for students at private schools.
A total of 68 per cent of foreign language-educated pupils have received Higher Grade A.
Data collected from 74 SCIS-affiliated schools showed that 72 per cent of Beijing language-daughter-daughter-daughter-daughter-daughter-daughter-grade A-dwellers, while 72 per cent of German-speaking students, 69 per cent of French-speaking students, and 63 per cent of those who learn Spanish also earned A.
This shows that private schools in Scotland are encouraging foreign language learning as an important skill that children and young people will definitely need in the future.
Currently, the language is treated as an elective in the same way as STEM subjects (science, science, technology, engineering, and mathematics) in curricula of private schools.
Seventeen per per cent of the reasons employers have struggled to bury vacancies were due to a lack of language skills.
Therefore, language skills have become increasingly essential for young people to prepare for future careers.
In a situation where there are more opportunities for jobs that require language due to globalization, these skills are essential.
No matter what kind of career you choose, if you’re in a foreign language, you’ve acquired these lifelong skills, and it’s going to be really beneficial in the future.
Communicating face-to-face with people in foreign countries means that multi-lingual people have the upper hand in the competition.
According to YouGov’s survey of more than 4,000 British adults in 2013, 75 per cent did not have enough foreign language skills to have a conversation, and a ratio of double-digit speakers was only 15 per cent French.
That’s why investing in language education is so important for today’s children.
Especially for children in developing countries, having multi-lingual skills will make them more likely to gain meaningful jobs.
In Scotland, the teaching language is different for each school.
Many schools will focus on more traditional modern languages, but others will teach languages that are considered most important to the UK, such as Mandarin and Japanese.
No matter what the child is interested in, there are plenty of language options in private schools where there are specialized teachers in the field.
Private schools in Scotland are focused on providing a learning environment in which their children can equip themselves with the skills they need to be prepared for the future and to be successful, no matter what the future holds.
In a global business environment, language will continue to be critical to the country’s future, so it needs to be reflected in education.
In fact, modern languages should be seen as “international communication skills.”
Private schools will continue to provide this choice, diversity, and better education to young people in Scotland.
Il faut bien le faire (which should be done)
John Edward is a member of the Scottish Private Schools Council.
