“North Korea says there will be ‘never’ to disarm unilaterally without trust
North Korea’s foreign minister told the U.S. on Saturday that the ongoing sanction would increase distrust of the U.S. and that he would not unilaterally renounce his nuclear weapons under these circumstances.
Foreign Minister Lee Yong-ho said at the U.N.'s annual plenary session that North Korea had taken "important well-intentioned measures" over the past year, including a cessation of nuclear and missile tests, the dismantling of a nuclear test site and a pledge not to spread nuclear weapons and nuclear technology.
“But we don’t see a substantial response from the U.S.,” he said.
“If we don’t trust the U.S., our faith in our national security will be lost, and we won’t disarm unilaterally under these circumstances.”
Lee reiterated North Korea’s familiar dissatisfaction that the U.S. government lacked recompense for North Korea’s “step-to-stage” denuclearization approach, but his statement is meaningful in that, unlike in the past, the North Korean government did not immediately reject unilateral denuclearization.
At the first summit between the U.S. president and the North Korean leader held in Singapore on June 12th, Li promised that Kim Jong-un would work towards "the denuclearization of the Korean Peninsula," referring to a joint statement in which Trump pledged North Korea's security.
North Korea is calling for a formal end to the 1950-53 Korean War, but the U.S. says North Korea needs to give up its nuclear weapons first.
The U.S. government also resisted a call to ease severe international sanctions against North Korea.
"The U.S. advocates 'denuclearization first' and opposes 'declaration of the end-of-the-life of the war,' with sanctions in order to forcibly achieve its objectives," Li said.
“The perception that sanctions can succumb to us is an illusion of those who do not know us.
However, the problem is that the continuation of the sanctum of the sanctum of us is intensifying.”
Li did not mention plans for a second summit between Kim and Trump, which President Trump emphasized at the United Nations earlier this week.
"The denuclearization of the denuclearization of the Korean Peninsula would not have reached such a dead end if the denuclearization parties were South Korea, not the U.S. and South Korean President Moon Jae-in for the past five months."
Even so, the tone of Li's speech is dramatically different from when Kim called Trump a "president of evil" and made it inevitable for North Korean rockets to reach the U.S. mainland during last year's United Nations General Assembly after Trump called gold a "Rocket Man" in a self-inflicted attack.
Trump, who threatened to “completely destroy” North Korea last year, praised gold at the United Nations this year for its courage in reaching an effort to disarm, but he said there were a number of challenges and that sanctions would need to be maintained until North Korea denuclearized.
On Wednesday, Trump said, "It doesn't matter if it's going to take two or three years, it doesn't matter if it's for five months."
Both China and Russia have disputed that the U.N. Security Council should reward the measures taken by North Korea.
But U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo told the United Nations Security Council on Thursday: "The Security Council Sanctions must continue strictly until they are fully and finally denuclearized."
The Security Council unanimously tightened sanctions on North Korea to cut off funds for North Korea's nuclear and ballistic missile programs.
Pompeo had a meeting with Foreign Minister Lee at the time of the United Nations General Assembly and said he would visit Pyongyang again next month in preparation for a second summit.
Pompeo has already visited North Korea three times this year this year, but his last visit was unsuccessful.
When he left Pyongyang in July, he said progress had been made, but within a few hours he accused North Korea of making a "gang-like request."
In a meeting with President Moon this month, North Korea pledged to demolish its missile and nuclear facilities if the U.S. "responsible measures" take "corresponding measures."
He says Kim told him that the “responsible measure” he was seeking was Trump’s promised security in Singapore and a move toward normalizing relations with the U.S. government.
Harvard students take courses to get plenty of rest
This year’s new course at Harvard University aims to ensure that all undergraduates get more sleep in order to combat the expansion of the macho culture of taking caffeine and studying on “all-night.”
The world’s best Harvard students are often ignorant of very basic things, such as how to take care of the body.
Charles Zeissler, a professor of sleep medicine at the Harvard University School of Medicine and a specialist at Brigham and Women's Hospital, designed the course, which he says is believed to be the U.S.'s first such course.
After giving a lecture on the impact of sleep deprivation on learning, he came up with the idea of starting this course.
“Finally, a female student came up to me and said, “Why do I finally hear this story when I’m a fourth-year student?”
I was surprised that no one had ever explained the importance of sleep to her," he told Telegraph.
The course, which was held for the first time this year, provides students with key points on how good sleeping habits can promote their learning and motor skills and improve general well-being.
Paul Bareira, professor of psychiatry at the Harvard School of Medicine and the director-general of the university’s medical services, says the university decided to introduce the course after it became aware of a severe sleep deprivation among students that week.
A one-hour course includes a series of interactive tasks.
In one of the sections, students can learn about coffee cups, curtains, sneakers, and books in their dormitories to learn about the effects of caffeine and light, the effects of sleep deprivation on motor skills, and the importance of daily routines on bedtime.
In a separate section, the participants described how long-term sleep deprivation may promote the risk of heart attack, stroke, depression, and cancer.
In addition, the campus map with interactive icons encourages participants to think about their daily routines.
“We know that the behavior of the students will not change immediately.
However, they must have the right to know, just as they have the right to know how the choice of smoking affects their health,” Zeissler adds.
A culture of “all-night study” still exists, he said, adding that sleep deprivation problems are growing due to increased pressure on modern technology and students.
A good enough night’s sleep is a “secret weapon” for students battling stress, exhaustion, and anxieties, he said, preventing weight gain because sleep deprivation puts the brain into a starvation mode and keeps the brain hungry all the time.
Raymond Thor, a (19-year-old from California who teaches chemistry and physicobiology), who studied chemistry and physicology, helped design his courses after taking Professor Zeissler's class at Harvard last year when he was in his first year.
The course awakened and wanted to expand the course to the entire campus.
The next step he wants is to make sure that every graduate student undergoes a similar learning program before they begin to study at Harvard, a competitive place.
Professor Zeissler urges students to set an alarm at bedtime, as well as wake-up times, and to be aware of the harmful "blue light" effects of circadian rhythm due to poor circadian rhythm and lead to falling asleep.
