HIV/AIDS: New cases soared by 14% in China
China has announced a 14 per cent increase in the number of citizens infected with HIV and AIDS.
More than eighty-two million people in the country are affected, health officials say.
In the second quarter of 2018 alone, nearly 4 million new cases were reported.
The vast majority of the new cases were transmitted through sexual intercourse, and there have been signs of change from the past.
In former China, HIV has spread rapidly in some areas as a result of a blood transfusion.
However, Chinese health officials at a meeting in Yunnan province reported that the number of people infected with HIV in this way fell to almost zero.
However, the number of people infected with HIV and AIDS in China has increased by one hundred thousand on a year-on-year basis.
In the LGBT community in China, HIV infection due to sexual intercourse has become a serious problem.
In China, homosexuality was legalized in 1997, but discrimination against LGBT people is said to be rife.
According to the survey, due to the country's conservative values, 70 to 90 per cent of gay men will eventually marry a woman.
Many infections occur due to the lack of preventative measures in these relationships.
Ever since 2003, the Chinese government has pledged to make anti-HIV drugs more widely accessible as part of its efforts to tackle the issue.
