What is the Global Citizen's Festival and has it helped reduce poverty?
In New York on this Saturday, there will be an annual music event, the Global Citizen's Festival, a music festival, in New York, where there are a lot of very impressive stars to perform, but what is equally impressive is the mission of ending the world's poverty.
This year marks its 7th year at the Global Citizen Festival, where tens of thousands of people will not only enjoy performances by Janet Jackson, Cardi B and Shawn Mendez in the Central Park lawn plaza, but will also gather to raise awareness of the event’s true goal of ending extreme poverty by 2030.
The Global Citizen's Festival, which began in 2012, is an extension of the Global Poverty Project, an international rights advocacy organization dedicated to ending poverty by increasing the number of people actively fighting.
In order to receive free tickets for the event (unless you want to buy a VIP ticket), the concert participants had to volunteer, e-mail to world leaders, make a phone call, or otherwise complete a series of “actions” in a meaningful way that would raise a sense of purpose of ending poverty.
But with twelve more years to go, to what extent has the Global Citizen’s Festival achieved that goal so far?
The idea of persuading people to take action with the reward of a free-to-concert is an honest way, or is it another case of “clickivism” that just makes you feel like you’ve done something meaningful by signing or tweeting an online petition?
