Inventors of the World Wide Web plan to launch a new internet alternative to Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, the inventor of the World Wide Web, has set up a start-up business to become a rival for Facebook, Amazon and Google.
Inrupt is the latest project by technology legend Lee and consists of Berners-Lee’s open-source open-source platform “Solid” as a board.
Solid gives users the option of where the data is stored and who has access to what information.
In an exclusive interview with Fast, Berners-Lee joked that Inrupt’s hidden intentions were “world domination.”
As for the start-of-the-art company, he says, “This is what we need to do right now.”
“This is a historic moment.”
The app uses Solid’s technology to make it possible to create your own personal online data store (POD).
This includes contact lists, to-do lists, calendars, music libraries, and other personal and professional tools.
Like Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack, and Spotify, it can be used from a single browser at the same time.
The unique thing about a personal online data store is that it is entirely up to the user who has access to what information.
Inrupt calls it “individual empowerment through data.”
According to Inrupt’s CEO, Inrupt’s idea is to provide resources, processes, and the right skills to make Solid available to everyone.
Inrupt currently consists of Berners-Lee, Bruce, a security platform acquired by IBM, an on-the-staff developer who contracted for a project, and a community of volunteer coders.
Starting this week, technology developers from all over the world will be able to create their own decentralized apps using the tools available on the Inrupt website.
“We’re talking about how he and his team are going to introduce a complete change that the business model between Facebook and Google will turn upside down overnight,” Berners-Lee said.
“I’m not asking them for permission.”
In a media announcement on Saturday, Berners-Lee said of Inrupt: "Our mission is to provide commercial energy and an ecosystem that ensures the integrity and quality of the new web that will be built on Solid."
Berners-Lee founded the World Wide Web Consortium at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology in 1994, which revolutionized the internet.
Over the past few months, Berners-Lee has continued to make influential remarks amid the net neutrality debate.
Berners-Lee retains his position as the founder and director of the World Wide Web Consortium, the Web Foundation, and the Open Data Institute, even during the launch of Inrupt.
“I’m very optimistic about this next-generation website,” Berners-Lee added.
Barnard Van: First World War Victoria's Cross, a celebratory ceremony is held to honor the clergy.
As a combatant, a celebratory ceremony was held in the town of his one-hundred-year-old homeland in connection with the clergyman of the Church of England, who won the Victorian Cross during the First World War.
Lt. Col. Bernard Van, a pastor, earned him a medal on September 29, 1918, by the Battle of Berndriez and Roocourt.
Four days later he was killed by a sniper and did not know that he had the highest honor as a British soldier.
On Saturday, a parade was taking place in Lashden, Norsempshire, where a commemorative skeletal monument has been opened by his two grandchildren.
One of his great-natured grandsons, Michael Vann, said the sequel was "outrageously symbolic" and that just a hundred-year-old has just opened to the public since his brilliance that his grandfather was given a medal.
According to the London Gazette, on September 29, 1918, Lt. Col. Van led a battalion through a battalion through Canal de Saint-Kantan through "a very thick fog and a fierce fire from the front and machine guns."
After that, with "great courage" at the forefront of gunfire, he rushed with a rifle in one hand, pushed back the front line, and repulsed the three platoons.
Lt. Col. Van was killed by a German sniper on October 4, 1918, just a month before the end of the war.
Seventy-seven-year-old Michael Van said of his grandfather’s actions: “It’s something I can’t imitate, but it’s humiliating.”
He and his older brother, Dr. James Vann, held a grand congratulatory congratulation after a parade led by the Brentwood Royal Youth Band.
"It was a tremendous honor to participate in the parade," Michael Van said, adding that "the courage of a true hero has been demonstrated with the support of so many people."
