Germany's New Military Delayed Again
The German navy planned to renovate a Cold War-era-era warship into a state-of-the-art frifrigate in 2014, but due to systemic problems and ever-incredulating costs, local media reported.
According to Di Zeit, citing an army spokesman's remark, the start-up of Baden-württemberg-class frigate's lead ship, the Ryland-Faltz, has been extended until the first half of 2019.
The battleships were scheduled to be introduced in 2014, but problems persisted after delivery, leading to a significant miscarriage of an ambitious plan.
The Baden-Württemberg-class frigate ordered by the Navy in 2007 is supposed to be used by the military in place of Bremen-class frigates, which are currently dilapidated.
The Baden-Württemberg-class frigate features powerful cannons, anti-aircraft and anti-ship missiles, as well as stealth technologies such as reduced-radar, infrared and acoustic signatures.
An other important feature is that the maintenance cycle has a long maintenance cycle, and it is possible to dispatch the frigate to a distance from its home port for up to two years.
However, due to the ongoing delays in the project, the Dee Zeit newspaper notes that modern warships, which should play an important role in Germany’s ability to show military power outside the country, will be obsolete by the time they actually begin to be in use.
The troubled F125 frigate became big news when the German navy officially refused to start last year and sent it back to Brome-Unt Phos in Hamburg.
This is the first time the Navy has returned a warship to a shipbuilding company after delivery.
There are no details about the causes behind the returns, but German media cites information that there were several serious "software and structural deficiencies" in which warships were incompatible with combat missions.
The Baden-Württemberg-class frigate is piloted by about one hundred and twenty marines, which is half of the personnel required for the Bremen-class a century-old Bremen-class, so the flaw in the software has a particularly significant effect.
The Baden-Württemberg-class frigates are also so heavy that their performance will be degraded, as well as limited upgrades that the Navy can perform in the future.
It weighs twice as much as a German-class ship used in World War II.
In addition to structural deficiencies, the overall cost of the project, including occupant training, has also emerged as an issue.
It is said that the overall cost of the project has ballooned from twenty-two euros to thirty-one to thirty-one to thirty-six euros to the present.
The issue of the latest frigate is of great importance, partly due to a warning that the German Navy's forces are said to be waning.
Since the beginning of this year, Hans Peter Bartels, head of the German parliament's defence committee, has admitted that the Navy is actually "departing warships with enough capacity to deploy."
He said the problem has become increasingly severe over time because the use of old warships has been cessated but there are no replacement warships.
He also regretted the situation in which a Baden-Württemberg-class frigate would not be introduced to the Navy at all.
The National Trust closes in on the mystery of bats
A new study being carried out on a site in Scotland’s highlands aims to shed light on how bats use the terrain when preying.
This study is expected to provide new information on the behavior of this unique flying mammal and contribute to future conservation efforts.
This study, conducted by researchers at the National Trust for Scotland, tracked species such as general bats, soprano pyistrels, soprano pipisteles, Brown Dongwooer, and Dobenton at Inverwe Gardens in Westerros.
A dedicated recorder will be installed at the main points of the premises to track bat activity throughout the season.
NHS staff and volunteers will also conduct mobile surveys using portable detectors.
Every recorded sound is analyzed by an expert to determine what species are doing in addition to the frequency of the call emitted by bats.
Habitat maps and reports will be produced based on the analysis, and the behavior of bats will be drawn in detail according to the terrain.
The NTS Nature Conservation Adviser, Rob Dewer, hopes that the study will shed light on what is the most important habitat for bats and how their habitats are being used by a variety.
This information can be useful in determining the benefits of habitat management operations, such as how to place habitats for bats and related species and how to protect forests.
In Scotland and throughout the United Kingdom, bat populations have declined significantly over the past hundred years.
Bats are at risk of extinction due to construction and development works that affect the condition of perches, as well as habitat loss.
In addition to chemoccurring paper and building materials for smuggling, attacks from pet cats, as well as windmills and lighting, are also a risk for bats.
In reality, bats are not blind.
However, because bats are nocturnal, they have a habit of relying more on hearing during predating activities.
Bats make full use of their developed reverberant positioning abilities to pinpoint insects and recognize obstacles in flight routes.
The NTS, which manages 270 historic buildings, 38 significant gardens and more than 76,000 hectares of property in the country, takes bats very seriously.
The NTS has ten experts who conduct regular surveys to inspect the bat’s mortuary trees and sometimes protect bats.
The NTS is also Scotland’s first protected area dedicated to bats on the Three Estates of Damflies and Galloway, which is home to eight out of ten Scottish bat species.
According to David Thompson, who manages the estate, the area is an ideal environment for bats.
"The sleeve here is the best place for bats," he said.
"There's an old building here, there's a lot of old trees, and they're all part of a suitable habitat for bats.
But there's still a lot we don't know about bats, so activities here and in other places can help us understand what's needed to do to breed bats.'
He notes that it’s important to check the bats before carrying out conservation work on the land, because if a bat accidentally destroys even a single tree they’re raising, it would kill up to four hundred female bats and young bats, which could lead to the extermination of the land’s bats, so it’s important to see bats before they can carry out conservation work in the land.
Bats have been designated as a protective animal, and killings, abusing, interfering, or cutting down household trees is illegal.
Elisabeth Ferrell, a member of the Scottish branch of the bat protection trust, is seeking support from people in the area.
"There's still so much to learn about Scottish bats, and we don't really really know how they're behaving," he said.
