british Britain 'would regret forever' for loss of auto industry
Business Minister Greg Clark said the UK "would forever regret" lose its status as a world-leading car manufacturer due to Brexit.
He was also "concerned" that the UK's Toyota told the BBC that it would suspend production at its Bernaston plant if the UK were to leave the EU without an agreement.
"An agreement is needed," Clarke said.
Japan’s Japanese automaker says a Brexit without a deal would result in a loss for the business.
At Toyota's Bernaston plant, "Auris" and "Avensis" were manufactured, and 90 per cent of the 150,000 units produced last year were exported to European Union countries outside the UK.
Marvin Cook, director of Toyota’s Bernaston plant, said: “If the U.K. forcibly leaves the EU by the end of March, manufacturing at the plant will be halted.”
Automakers in the UK, including Honda, BMW and Jaguar Land Rover, have also raised concerns about a no-deal Brexit on cross-border transactions.
As an example, BMW plans to shut down the "mini" factory where the company operates in Oxford for a month after Brexit.
At the heart of automakers’ concerns are the supply chain risks posed by a no-deal Brexit.
Toyota's production lines are based on "just-in-time," and bespoke vehicles use parts that arrive every 37 minutes from suppliers in the UK and within the EU.
According to the automotive manufacturing industry, the UK's departure from the EU without an agreement on March 29 could lead to delays at the border, leading to delays in the arrivals of parts and delayed or underachievement.
Toyota says it will not be possible to maintain more than a day’s worth of inventory at the company’s Derbyshire plant, which will stop manufacturing.
Mr Clarke said the "Chequers Plan," which Prime Minister Theresa May presented towards his future relationship with the EU, was "enoughly crafted so that we can evade checks at the borders that the industry is concerned about."
“There needs to be an agreement, which means we need the best agreement not only to enjoy its current success, but also to take advantage of this event as an opportunity,” he said on the BBC Radio 4 Today programme.
“Not only Toyota but also other manufacturers are demonstrating that we need to be able to completely continue the supply chain, which has worked very well to date.”
Toyota says it’s unclear how long it’s going to stop manufacturing, but in the long-term, additional costs will make the plant less competitive and ultimately include jobs.
Peter Tsouvaralis, who has worked at the Bernaston plant for twenty-four years and is the head of a labor union union at the plant, said there are growing concerns among union members: "In terms of the experience, there will be no job openings again if there are no jobs like this.
A government spokeswoman said: "The government is presenting an accurate and credible plan for the future relationship between the UK and the EU."
“Trump and Rosenstein’s meeting may be postponed again,” the White House said.
A planned high-level meeting between U.S. President Donald Trump and Deputy Attorney General Rob Rosenstein could be postponed "one more week" due to the ongoing conflict over Supreme Court Justice Brett Kavanaugh's candidacy, the White House announced on Sunday.
Rosenstein oversees the work of Special Agent Robert Mueller, who is conducting an investigation into Russian election meddling, the connection between President Trump’s entourage and Russia, and obstruction of justice by the president.
Over the past few months, there has been a lot of attention from around Washington about whether President Trump’s deputy attorney general will be removed and Morrah’s independence will be affected.
