The U.S.-China trade war benefits in Beijing.
The trade war between the U.S. and China began with a one-on-a-court attack and is still far from an end, but rifts between the two countries could benefit Beijing in the long run, experts say.
U.S. President Donald Trump earlier this year issued a warning by taxing major Chinese exports, including solar panels, steel and aluminum.
The largest tariffs made this week will affect items worth up to $20bn (£15bn) due to the new levy, and will be taxed by half of the products imported from China to the United States.
Beijing retaliates in the same way each time, most recently taking five to ten-percent levies on a six-hundred-billion-billion-dollar U.S. product.
Each time, China swore an oath against the U.S., and the world’s second-largest economy is not expected to back down anytime soon.
Surrendering to Washington means surrender, but obeying the U.S. would be a major humiliation for China’s supreme leader, Xi Jinping.
Still, experts say that if Beijing handled the card correctly, by curbing the interdependence of the two economies, the pressures of a U.S. trade war could positively support China in the long run.
“The fact that an early political decision by Washington or Beijing creates a state in which economic turbulence begins in both countries is more dangerous than observers previously thought,” said Abigail-Grace, an Asian-focused research associate at the think tank’s New America Security Center.
Syria's foreign minister is 'ready' for refugee return
Syria says it is ready for a self-imposed repatriation of refugees and appeals for help in the reconstruction of a country ravaged by a seven-year-old war.
According to the United Nations General Assembly, Foreign Minister Warrido al-Murem said the country's condition was improving.
“Thanks to the progress of the struggle against terrorism, the situation on the ground is becoming more stable and more secure,” he said.
The government is continuing to repair areas that have been devastated by terrorists in an effort to restore normal bodies.
All situations are now prepared for refugees who voluntarily return to their countries, which had to be evacuated due to terrorism and biased economic measures for their daily lives and livelihoods.
The United Nations estimates that more than 5.5 million Syrians have been evacuated since the start of the war in 2011.
The other six million people still live in the country and are in need of humanitarian assistance.
Al-Murem said the Syrian regime welcomes support for the reconstruction of the collapsed country.
However, he stressed that he would not accept conditional assistance or support from countries sponsoring the civil war.
